+# CI Triage — #1811: Secret Scanning red on PR #1810 (run 40602662180)
+
+> **Task ID:** `bridge-20261028-run11-wo1811-secrets-scan-triage`
+> **Claimed:** 2026-10-28T01:39:49Z by zai-mcp-tower-agent (cron Job 477795, run 11)
+> **Scope:** docs-only triage evidence, per the WO's own lane contract — the failure
+> lives on another agent's bridge branch, so board agents deliver evidence and a
+> rescue recipe, not a main-side code fix.
+
+## 1. Verdict
+
+Real failure, content-triggered, PR-lane, blockage ongoing 2h+ at triage time.
+This is **not** a stale alarm (contrast the #1804 wave): PR #1810 is still open
+with a red Secret Scanning check as of 01:22Z, and the bridge cannot land it
+while the constitutional gate is red. The failing artifact is a docs-only
+federation audit report whose own evidence literals trip the org secret rules.
+
+## 2. Evidence chain (all live reads / local repro, 2026-10-28T01:22–01:37Z)
+
+| # | Fact | Source |
+|---|------|--------|
+| E1 | Run 40602662180, job Secret Scanning, exit code 1, 2026-10-27T23:31:29Z, PR #1810 lane (ref 1810/merge) | github_get_failed_logs + run-page annotation |
+| E2 | PR #1810 is docs-only: docs/reports/FEDERATION_AUDIT_2026-10-28.md, +183 lines, taskId bridge-20261028-federation-audit; still open | public PR page |
+| E3 | The secrets-scan job checks out fetch-depth 0 and calls the reusable composite action with default log-opts origin/main..HEAD — i.e. it scans only the commits the PR itself introduces | pipeline.yml sha d46b52c9cfb4; action.yml sha e36fb1476c80 |
+| E4 | Org config .gitleaks.toml (sha 447ee752b48d, issue #41) extends upstream with org rules; the cloudflare-api-token rule matches ANY 40-char word; the cloudflare-global-key rule matches 37+ lowercase hex | .gitleaks.toml on main |
+| E5 | **Local repro** (decisive): reconstructed the 183-line doc from the PR diff into a fresh git repo, ran gitleaks v8.18.4 (same version CI installs) with the same flags. Without the org config: 0 findings. With the repo .gitleaks.toml: **6 findings** (2 org rules x 3 full-length SHAs on doc lines 5–6) | executed locally, output captured |
+| E6 | The 3 flagged literals are git commit SHAs — main 961123f7781, studio a39c13415ce, vscode 6ce2dc294a8 (truncated here on purpose; see §6). The rules' keyword pre-filter is satisfied by the word cloudflare appearing elsewhere in the doc (the package inventory names cloudflare-studio-proxy) | PR diff + repro output |
+| E7 | Counter-evidence that the job is NOT globally broken: PR-lane run 4319 (PR #1876) passed Secret Scanning at 01:20Z; main-lane runs 4318/4320 are green | github_workflow_runs |
+
+## 3. Root cause (two layers)
+
+**Layer 1 — content.** The audit doc quotes three full-length 40-hex commit
+SHAs as parity evidence. Two org rules match that shape (37+ hex; any 40-char
+word), so the fail-closed gate exits 1 and the job goes red. Whether a given
+40-hex string actually flags depends on an accident of keyword proximity: the
+word cloudflare must appear somewhere in the scanned fragment. Same-shaped
+SHAs in other landed docs (e.g. the run-10 quota wiring doc pins three
+full-length action SHAs) sailed through only because their files never say
+cloudflare.
+
+**Layer 2 — process.** .gitleaksignore on main already carries THREE
+fingerprints for this exact file under issue #1763, pinned to commit
+b886c118154e. PR #1810's re-submission carries a different commit, so every
+pinned fingerprint silently misses — commit-pinned ignore entries do not
+survive re-submission, amend, or rebase. Two further gaps in the #1763 batch:
+it covers only 3 of the 4 fingerprints the current content needs (the
+global-key match on line 5 was never covered), and two of the three SHAs sit
+on one line, where a single file:line:rule fingerprint keying cannot
+distinguish them (one entry happens to cover both, by luck of the format).
+
+## 4. Rescue recipe for the PR #1810 author lane (pick one)
+
+**Option A — recommended, rebase-proof.** Truncate the three SHAs to 12 hex
+inside the doc. Evidence value is preserved (12 hex is standard short-SHA
+practice; the run-7 sentinel manifest and the run-9/10 bridge docs already
+follow this convention). Both org rules stop matching structurally, and no
+ignore entry is needed. Then re-run the check — the scan range covers only
+the PR's own commits, so one clean commit clears the job.
+
+**Option B — fragile, works today, dies on the next rebase.** Append fresh
+.gitleaksignore entries keyed to the PR's CURRENT commit: 4 entries
+(api-token on lines 5 and 6, global-key on lines 5 and 6). The file is
+allowlisted inside both org rules, so the entries themselves do not trip the
+scan. But this is exactly the mechanism that silently broke between #1763 and
+#1810 — every future amend regenerates the commit sha and the red returns.
+
+## 5. Systemic recommendations (refs #1825 #1777 #1801 #1804 #1868)
+
+1. **Standing convention — quote SHAs at 12 hex in public-repo docs, never
+   40.** The 40-hex shape collides with the org cloudflare rules, and flagging
+   is keyword-proximity-dependent. 65 doc-ish files on main currently carry
+   40-hex tokens; at least docs/plans/plan_registry.json (50 hits) sits one
+   stray cloudflare keyword away from the same red on its next PR lane.
+   Prevention beats per-incident ignore archaeology.
+2. **Lane classification (feeds #1825).** Add the incident class
+   secrets-scan-red-on-docs with disposition fix-in-author-lane plus the
+   rescue recipe above; the auto-mirrored incident (#1811) is then
+   P2-triage-closure, not a code hunt on main.
+3. **Watchdog evidence gap.** The failed-log window the CI incident mirror
+   captures shows the job tail (post-cleanup), not the gitleaks findings, so
+   every triage agent must rebuild a local repro to recover rule IDs. A small
+   action.yml change (print the findings fingerprint list to the job summary)
+   is admin-lane (.github/ protected) and would make this class self-triaging.
+4. **Ignore-entry hygiene.** When a .gitleaksignore batch is added, derive it
+   from a live scan of the exact content, not from an earlier commit's list —
+   the #1763 batch was both incomplete and commit-pinned, which is why the
+   same content re-flagged within hours.
+
